Jonathan Goldberg-Hiller; hiller@hawaii.edu

The Department of Physics & Astronomy at Vanderbilt University invites applications for a tenure-track Assistant Professor position in high-energy nuclear theory. We are particularly interested in candidates addressing fundamental questions about strongly interacting matter, from the emergence of collective behavior to the short-distance structure and dynamics of dense QCD matter. Areas of interest include QCD at finite temperature and density, systems near critical points and far from equilibrium, and probes of QCD matter across multiple scales. Approaches may include effective field theory and perturbative QCD, relativistic hydrodynamics and transport, lattice field theory, and quantum simulations.
The successful candidate will join a vibrant high-energy nuclear physics community at Vanderbilt, including a strong experimental program, offering significant opportunities for intellectual exchange across nuclear physics and related fields.
Responsibilities
Establish and lead an independent, externally funded research program;
Contribute to undergraduate and graduate teaching in physics;
Support the Arts & Science Core Curriculum;
Mentor graduate students and postdoctoral scholars;
Engage with Vanderbilt’s interdisciplinary research community and external partners, including Oak Ridge National Laboratory.
Qualifications
• Ph.D. in Physics or a closely related field, awarded no later than August 1, 2027.
• Demonstrated research excellence in theoretical physics, with expertise in one or more areas represented in the search;
• A strong research program addressing important questions in high-energy nuclear theory;
• Evidence of, or clear potential for, securing external research funding;
• Commitment to excellence in teaching and mentorship at all levels.
Application Instructions
Application Materials
Interested candidates should submit the following as a single PDF, or as specified by the application portal:
1. Cover letter;
2. Curriculum vitae, including a complete publication list;
3. Research statement (maximum 3 pages) summarizing key contributions and future research directions;
4. Teaching statement addressing classroom philosophy, curriculum development, and student/postdoctoral mentorship. Evidence of teaching effectiveness (e.g., course evaluations) must be included, if available;
5. Applicants should arrange for three letters of confidential recommendation to be uploaded via the same link.
How to Apply
Applications should be submitted via https://apply.interfolio.com/193598.
Review Timeline: Review of applications will begin November 1st, 2026 and continue until the position is filled. For full consideration, please ensure all materials (including letters) are submitted by October 31st, 2026. Inquiries should be addressed to Professor Sait Umar: sait.a.umar@vanderbilt.edu.
EEO Statement
Vanderbilt University shall abide by the requirements of 41 CFR §§ 60-1.4(a), 60-300.5(a) and 60-741.5(a). These regulations prohibit discrimination against qualified individuals based on their status as protected veterans or individuals with disabilities and prohibit discrimination against all individuals based on their race, color, religion, sex, or national origin. Moreover, these regulations require that covered prime contractors and subcontractors provide equal employment opportunity without regard to race, color, religion, sex, national origin, protected veteran status or disability.

Job Details
The Department of Kinesiology and Health Promotion invites applications for an Assistant or Associate Professor position in Exercise Science. The position is Regular Title Series, tenure-eligible. This is a 9-month appointment, effective August 16, 2027.
The selected candidate will be expected to be actively committed to our departmental vision and mission by pursuing excellence in teaching, research and scholarship, and service. Successful candidates will be expected to teach graduate and undergraduate exercise science courses, with faculty in this position typically expected to teach a 2-2 course load.
Responsibilities include the following:
Teach 2 courses each semester (fall and spring), such as undergraduate courses in Strength and Conditioning for Sports, Physiology of Exercise, Introduction to Exercise Testing and Prescription, Practicum in Kinesiology and Health Promotion, Laboratory Methods in Exercise Science, or other exercise science/exercise physiology courses as assigned.
Provide support for the undergraduate Exercise Science and Strength and Conditioning major and minor programs;
Develop and maintain an active, interdisciplinary research agenda involving human subjects, showcasing a strong record of academic scholarship and potential for extramural support, as demonstrated by an independent research trajectory, peer-reviewed research publications, presentations, and/or grant support;
Advise and mentor master’s and doctoral students in the exercise science program;
Seek and be competitive for extramural research funding; and
Provide service to the department, college, university, and profession.
Interested applicants should apply at www.uky.edu/ukjobs and include the following materials:
a detailed cover letter noting academic credentials and professional experience related to the responsibilities for this position — upload under Cover Letter;
a curriculum vita (no photos) — upload under Curriculum Vita;
a statement on teaching that includes reflections on the candidate’s teaching experiences, mentoring undergraduate and graduate students, and copies of evaluations of the candidate’s teaching, if available — upload under Specific Request 1;
a statement on research describing the candidate’s research interests and areas of expertise, dissemination of research, and current/potential funding agencies — upload under Specific Request 2;
writing samples – up to three representative publications (first or corresponding author preferred) — upload under Writing Sample;
names and contact information for three professional references – upload under References. References will be contacted to upload a letter once finalists have been selected.
Review of applications will be conducted on a rolling basis. Preference will be given to applicants who have submitted their applications by October 16, 2026.
Questions regarding the position or application materials may be directed to the search committee chair Dr. Lance Bollinger (lance.bollinger@uky.edu).
Employment at UK is subject to fund availability and comes with many rewards, focusing on your total well-being and career development, with leadership striving to provide a strong work-life integration. The university generously contributes to employees’ retirement plans, medical coverage, and life insurance. In addition, UK offers optional benefits such as dental and vision insurance, additional retirement plans and much more. To learn more about these benefits, please visit: https://hr.uky.edu/employment/our-benefits.

The Department of East Asian Languages and Literatures of the University of Hawai'i at Mānoa invites applications for a full time, tenure-track position in Japanese Language Pedagogy at the level of Assistant Professor to start Fall 2027, pending position clearance and availability of funds.
The Department of EALL is among the largest departments of its kind in the country and offers a curriculum unparalleled in its breadth and scope in Chinese, Japanese, and Korean language, linguistics, literature, and culture. We offer B.A. degrees in Chinese, Japanese, and Korean; MA & PhD in East Asian Languages and Literatures, with concentrations on Chinese, Japanese, and Korean Linguistics and Literature.
Our campus welcomes all students but given our responsibilities to the community in which we operate, as part of our strategic mission, we strive to become a Native Hawaiian Place of Learning, a place that is committed to integrating Native Hawaiian language, culture and/or values into its academic and campus environment.
Duties and Responsibilities:
Teach undergraduate content courses and Japanese language courses (introductory to advanced);
Teach graduate courses in area of specialization;
Advise and mentor undergraduate and graduate students;
Serve on thesis/dissertation committees;
Maintain an active agenda of scholarly research and publication in area(s) of specialization;
Contribute service at the department, college, university, and community levels;
Other duties as assigned by the Chair.
Minimum Qualifications:
Ph.D. or equivalent degree in Japanese Language, Japanese Linguistics, or closely related field from a college or university of recognized standing (ABD will be considered; all requirements for degree must be completed by August 2027);
Demonstrated experience in teaching at the college level;
Demonstrated expertise in Japanese pedagogy;
Evidence of scholarly activity in the field of specialization;
High level of proficiency in Japanese and English;
Demonstrated ability to work effectively and collegially with students, faculty, staff and administrators.
Desirable Qualifications:
Ability to develop courses in interdisciplinary fields;
Demonstrated experience in mentoring and service at the college level;
Research capability in an additional area of Japanese linguistics beyond Japanese Pedagogy
Review of applications will begin December 15, 2026, and continue until the position is filled.
For more information and to apply, follow this link: https://go.hawaii.edu/RqV

The Herschend School of Education at Gordon College invites applications for a full time, 12 month, faculty position at the rank of assistant, associate, or full professor to serve as founding director of the Childhood Trauma and Resilience Project. Position to begin August 2027. Rank will be commensurate with experience.
The Childhood Trauma and Resilience Project is a donor-funded initiative that builds on the Herschend School’s core commitments to the science of learning and the translation of research into evidence-based educational practice. Grounded in the Science of Resilience, The project seeks to equip K-12 educators to empower children and adolescents to flourish—both academically and as whole people—despite adversity and trauma through an emphasis on developing both educator and learner resilience.
This position offers a rare opportunity to help build a distinctive program connecting childhood adversity, resilience science, the science of learning, and educational practice to promote human flourishing. The founding director will provide intellectual and programmatic leadership for The Childhood Trauma and Resilience Project through an integrated program of teaching, scholarship, and service, helping to establish Gordon College as a national leader in translating resilience research into educational practice. Initial project priorities include the development of an online certificate program and new teacher induction seminar; execution of an applied research agenda; and engagement with school and community partners. Consistent with the project’s commitment to translating research to practice, teaching is a central responsibility of the position. Faculty workload will include 50-70% teaching and 30-50% scholarship, project leadership, partnership development, and outreach. Responsibilities will be adjusted annually in response to the developmental needs and strategic priorities of the project.
Gordon College is a Christian liberal arts college located in Massachusetts on Boston’s North Shore, a beautiful coastal area with easy access to Boston. Core to the Gordon College mission and curriculum are faculty commitments to integrate faith, learning, and living; cultivate imagination with a disposition of hope; and inspire creative contributions to God’s purposes. The Gordon College community has made a commitment to pursuing shalom, a right ordering of relationships and actions resulting in the affirmation of human dignity and the flourishing of community. We seek a colleague whose scholarship, teaching, and engagement with schools and communities will contribute to this vision.
Responsibilities:
• Provide mission-aligned intellectual and programmatic leadership for the Childhood Trauma and Resilience Project in collaboration with an interdisciplinary advisory group;
• Advance Gordon College’s distinctive contribution to the Science of Resilience through teaching, scholarship, partnership, and public engagement;
• Develop an online certificate program and new teacher induction seminar that translates research on childhood trauma, resilience, and educator resilience into actionable educational practices;
• Teach online courses with the option for in person courses where aligned with expertise and programmatic need;
• Lead collection and dissemination of evidence demonstrating project impact on educator knowledge, practice, and resilience;
• Serve as lead author on an annual report highlighting project outcomes, impact, and future priorities;
• Engage in purposeful service to the school, the institution, and the profession;
• Maintain an active scholarly research agenda;
• Develop partnerships with schools and other educational agencies.
Required Qualifications:
• A commitment to the Christian mission of the college and its commitment to integrating faith, learning, and living;
• Earned doctorate in education, psychology, child development, social work, or a closely related field;
• Established or emerging applied research program with clear emphasis on childhood trauma and resilience;
• Demonstrated ability to translate research into meaningful educational practice, including educator preparation, professional development, or school-based practice;
• A minimum of three years of experience in PK-12 education, educator preparation, or a closely related educational setting;
• Experience working with children, adolescents, and/or educators affected by adversity;
• Commitment to evidence-based practices equitably support all learners, including culturally and linguistically sustaining practices;
• Evidence of effective teaching, with preference for experience teaching in online settings;
• Strong interpersonal, communication, and collaborative skills, including the ability to work across traditional disciplinary and professional boundaries;
• Ability to collaborate effectively with internal and external partners.
Preferred Qualifications:
• A scholarly record informed by the Science of Resilience, Developmental Systems Theory, Positive Childhood Experiences, Trauma-Informed Practice or a related area with evidence of peer-reviewed publication, professional presentations, and practitioner-oriented resources;
• Demonstrated ability to develop and lead programs, initiatives, or collaborative projects;
• Experience designing online certificate or professional learning programs for educators;
• Evidence of thought leadership that reaches both scholarly and practitioner audiences;
• Experience working collaboratively with schools, districts, or other educational organizations;
• Experience securing external funding or developing grant proposals;
• Experience conducting applied or collaborative research with schools.
Interested Applicants Must Submit:
• Cover letter that addresses the applicant’s fit with the Christian mission of Gordon College and a rationale for interest in this specific position;
• CV that includes the names and contact information for 3 references;
• Teaching philosophy clearly informed by research on childhood trauma and resilience;
• A sample of academic writing.
Anticipated Salary Range: $69,917-$123,626 commensurate with experience.
TO APPLY: https://www.gordon.edu/offices-services/human-resources/jobs/open-faculty-positions
Visit https://www.gordon.edu/offices-services/human-resources/for-prospective-employees for more information on Gordon College.
Our review of applicants will begin October 15 and continue until the position is filled. Gordon College is an equal opportunity employer. Qualified applicants for employment will receive consideration without discrimination on the basis of race, color, gender, age, national origin, or disability.

The Department of Second Language Studies seeks to hire a scholar who conducts research on multilingualism in professional/vocational/employment contexts. The person filling this vital role will contribute to the scholarly mission of the department through research, teaching, and service focused on how languages are used in workplaces and institutions outside of formal education and how people are prepared for a multilingual workforce. Ideal candidates will have (a) a background in sociolinguistics, language for specific or vocational purposes, forensic linguistics, or language policy and (b) interest in networking with local services, such as the state Office of Language Access or court interpreter services, in order to build capacity for language access for all residents of the state.
The department is committed to serving the educational and social needs of the highly multilingual State of Hawai‘i as well as to being a world leader in the study of second, foreign, and heritage language learning, teaching, use, and analysis. The Department of Second Language Studies offers a BA, an MA and a PhD in Second Language Studies as well as an Advanced Graduate Certificate. The University of Hawai‘i is a Carnegie very high research activity university with a strong orientation to the Asia-Pacific region. The University supports interdisciplinary initiatives within and across departments and colleges and places high value on extramural funding. It also values research that addresses locally-relevant issues and local populations in Hawai‘i.
Our campus welcomes all students but given our responsibilities to the community in which we operate, as part of our strategic mission, we strive to become a Native Hawaiian Place of Learning, a place that is committed to integrating Native Hawaiian language, culture and/or values into its academic and campus environment.
Duties and Responsibilities
Develop and teach courses at the undergraduate and graduate levels, including in the area of applied linguistics in multilingual workplaces and professional settings, based on the needs of the department;
Advise undergraduate and graduate students;
Serve on graduate committees;
Maintain an active agenda of scholarly research and publication;
Pursue opportunities for external funding;
Actively engage with ongoing and new campus and community initiatives, including university-community partnerships, that promote and study the development of a multilingual workforce for Hawaiʻi;
Participate in Second Language Studies program-building and other service work for the department, college, university, and broader communities;
Work collaboratively and collegially with diverse groups of students and faculty;
Other duties as assigned by the Chair.
Minimum Qualifications
PhD or equivalent degree in second language studies, applied linguistics, or a closely related field from a college or university of recognized standing; ABDs will be considered, but all requirements for doctoral degree must be completed by August 2027.
Demonstrated expertise in applied linguistics research in multilingual workplaces and professional contexts, as evidenced by publications or other scholarly contributions;
Demonstrated success in teaching;
Ability to develop and teach courses at the undergraduate and graduate levels;
Demonstrated ability to work effectively and collegially with diverse populations of students, faculty, staff and administrators.
Desirable Qualifications
Experience working with partners outside of academic settings on language-related topics (e.g., policy advising, community partnerships, language access or social service organizations, on-site field research)
Familiarity with Hawaiʻi’s multilingual and multicultural context.
Experience with internship program development, coordination, or supervision.
Ability to communicate scholarship beyond academia (e.g., in new media, blogs, etc.).
In addition to English, proficiency in one or more languages of contemporary relevance in the state of Hawaiʻi.
Expertise in one or more other areas that will broaden or enhance the strengths of our second language studies community, including but not limited to: sociolinguistics, linguistic discrimination, language policy, language for specific purposes (such as in law, tourism, business, or health professions), forensic linguistics, research methods (qualitative, quantitative, and/or mixed-methods), community organizing.
Demonstrated excellence in teaching, training, and supervision of undergraduate and graduate students.
Record of and/or demonstrated commitment to securing external funding.
Review of applications will begin November 9, 2026, and continue until the position is filled.
For more information and to apply, follow this link: https://go.hawaii.edu/RuF

The Department of Music at the University of Hawaiʻi at Mānoa has a long-standing reputation for excellence in the study of world music, especially the musics of Asia and the Pacific. The UHM Ethnomusicology program is well funded through several private endowments, with faculty and students receiving substantial support for research, conference travel and various other activities. The program is home to the Barbara Barnard Smith Endowed Chair in Ethnomusicology, a rotating position, with each ethnomusicology faculty member holding the chair for two years, during which time the holder receives additional salary and research support.
We seek a highly motivated individual with a commitment to excellence in teaching and a strong research agenda. Applicants should be prepared to participate in developing and implementing curricular and programmatic revisions in collaboration with current faculty and to build ties with colleagues in related disciplines and with the wider community.
Duties and Responsibilities
Teach undergraduate and graduate courses in ethnomusicology, related topics, and other areas of music as relevant to the candidate’s abilities, based on the needs of the department;
Maintain an active program of research, including publication and presentation at nationally and internationally recognized professional organizations/conferences;
Actively recruit graduate students;
Advise undergraduate and graduate students;
Serve on master’s and doctoral committees;
Work with colleagues to assist with curricular and program development;
Serve on department committees as assigned;
Provide service to the department, university, community, and field;
